Two tenants in one database: meridian has 2 runs, halvard has 1.

  scoped query      returned 2 rows to meridian   PASS
  missing WHERE     returned 3 rows to meridian   FAIL — 1 row(s) leaked

The isolation test is not 'does the query return data'. It is:

  ask as tenant A, assert that every row returned belongs to A

Write it once per table and run it on every build. It is three lines, it
catches a missing WHERE clause, and a missing WHERE clause is one of the
easiest ways for one customer to see another's data.

Two stronger versions, in increasing order of how much sleep they buy:

  a session variable    set the tenant on the connection and let the data
                        layer apply it, so forgetting is not possible
  row-level security    Postgres enforces the predicate itself, so a
                        hand-written query cannot cross — unless it runs
                        as a role the policy does not apply to

Both are more work than a WHERE clause and both remove a class of bug rather
than an instance of it, which is the trade worth making for exactly this one.
